# rdev: widen five private members of SwiftTerm.Terminal to internal so the read-only accessors in
# third_party/swiftterm/rdev_snapshot_access.swift (compiled into this module) can read them.
#
# SIX BEHAVIOUR FIXES, for fidelity with xterm.js 6 (the terminal serializer replays SwiftTerm's
# screen into xterm.js, so a state the two disagree on is a visible diff; the differential check is
# web/test/term-snapshot-fidelity.test.ts). Each is tested in Tests/SwiftTermAccessorTests.swift.
#
# 1. Column-operation gaps. DECIC (CSI ' }), DECDC (CSI ' ~) and DECBI/DECFI (ESC 6 / ESC 9) filled
#    their gap with `buffer.getNullCell()` — a written SPACE (scalar 32), and for DECIC/DECBI without
#    the erase background. xterm.js fills with its null cell (code 0, ERASED) carrying the erase
#    attribute, as SwiftTerm's own ICH/DCH already do. The four fills become
#    `CharData(attribute: eraseAttr())` (testColumnInsertAndDelete…).
# 2. Tab stops on a width change. SwiftTerm builds its buffers at 80 columns and then RESIZES them to
#    the requested width, and Buffer.resize appended columns with NO stops, so a 100-column terminal
#    had none past 72; a real resize also trimmed the stops past a narrower width and restarted the
#    defaults from the OLD width. xterm.js keeps stops in a map a shrink never trims and, after every
#    resize, restarts the defaults from the last stop below the NEW width (BufferSet.resize →
#    setupTabStops(newCols)); a new terminal and RIS give both buffers fresh defaults at the current
#    width. `setup()` now ends its resize with `setupTabStops()`, Buffer.resize only pads, and
#    Terminal.resize calls the new Buffer.rdevResizeTabStops in place of `setupTabStops(index: oldCols)`,
#    whose `oldCols` goes with it (testAWideTerminal…, testResizeSets…).
# 3. The alternate screen's fill. ?47h, ?1047h and ?1049h called `activateAltBuffer(fillAttr: nil)`;
#    xterm.js passes its erase attribute, so a fresh alt screen carries the pen's background. The
#    call passes `eraseAttr()` (testEnteringTheAltScreen…).
# 4. Leaving the alternate screen. xterm.js's ?47l, ?1047l and ?1049l ALL clear the alt buffer
#    (BufferSet.activateNormalBuffer), the clear restores its default tab stops (Buffer.clear →
#    setupTabStops), RIS gives a fresh alt buffer, and no alt-screen switch touches cursor
#    visibility. SwiftTerm kept ?47's contents (so the next ?47h showed them), kept the alt buffer's
#    stops, kept the alt buffer through RIS, and SHOWED the cursor on every switch (a TUI that hides
#    it before ?1049h got it back). The leave passes `clearAlt: true` and re-sets the alt stops, RIS
#    passes `clearAlt: true`, and the two `showCursor ()` calls go (testLeavingTheAltScreen…,
#    testAltScreenSwitchesLeaveCursorVisibilityAlone).
# 5. A recycled scrollback line. Once scrollback is full, Terminal.scroll RECYCLES the oldest line as
#    the new bottom line (CircularList.recycle), which erased its cells with the erase attribute but
#    KEPT its isWrapped and renderMode: every line that was a continuation when it was evicted planted
#    a stale wrap flag on a fresh bottom line, and a snapshot joined two unrelated lines with a run of
#    spaces (and SwiftTerm's own reflow joined them on a resize). xterm.js copies its blank line into
#    the recycled one (BufferService.scroll → recycle().copyFrom(newLine): cells, length, isWrapped).
#    The recycled line takes newLine's isWrapped and renderMode .single, or is replaced by a copy of
#    newLine if its length differs (testARecycledScrollbackLineIsAFreshUnwrappedLine; corpus case
#    recycled-wrapped-line).
# 6. Grapheme widths, as the client's xterm.js (the graphemes addon under rdev's provider,
#    web/src/client/term-unicode.ts) and tmux 3.7c count them — MEASURED 2026-09-27, docs/terminal.md
#    "Glyph widths". A lone regional indicator is ONE column (SwiftTerm: two) and the second of a
#    pair widens the cell to two; VS16 widens ANY base (SwiftTerm: only bases on its own list, and it
#    DROPPED the selector from any other); a skin-tone modifier widens its base (SwiftTerm kept ☝🏽
#    one column); VS15 narrows nothing (SwiftTerm narrowed ⌚︎ to one). A widening in the last column
#    moves the glyph to the next row and erases the cell it left with the pen, as xterm.js's print
#    does; under ?7l the joiner is dropped, as xterm.js drops it. And nothing joins a glyph across a
#    control or an escape sequence: xterm.js zeroes its join state on every EXECUTE, CSI, ESC, OSC and
#    DCS, where SwiftTerm joined to the last cell it wrote whatever came between; a joiner that joins
#    nothing stands alone, one column (two for a skin-tone modifier), where SwiftTerm dropped it.
#    Utilities.columnWidth loses its regional-indicator arm, the combining path in Terminal.handlePrint
#    is rewritten around a new `rdevJoinable` (cleared by printStateReset and dispatchExecute)
#    (testRegionalIndicators…, testVS16Widens…, testASkinToneModifier…, testAWideningAtTheMargin…,
#    testNothingJoinsAcrossAControlOrASequence).
#
# And FIVE HOST-SAFETY FIXES (7-11), not about fidelity — the host links SwiftTerm IN-PROCESS, so a
# crash or a hang here is one in the app, reachable by any program printing to a `pty:` tab:
# 7. A DECRQSS (`DCS $ q … ST`) whose data is not ASCII CRASHED the process: the handler logged
#    `newData!`, the data decoded with `.ascii`, which is nil for any byte >= 0x80 — and the host
#    links SwiftTerm in-process, so one `printf '\eP$q\xc3\xa9\e\\'` in a `pty:` tab killed the app.
#    SwiftTerm parses BYTES, so a DECRQSS ended by the C1 ST U+009C (bytes C2 9C) put the C2 in the
#    data and crashed it too (found MEASURING 8-bit C1, 2026-09-27, docs/terminal.md). The log line
#    reads `newData ?? "(not ASCII)"`; the switch already answers nil as invalid, `DCS 0 $ r ST`, which
#    is xterm.js's answer (testADECRQSSWithNonASCIIDataIsAnsweredNotACrash).
# 8. SIXEL is never decoded. `dispatchDcs` no longer returns a SixelDcsHandler for `DCS q`, so the DCS is
#    consumed and ignored — which is what the client's xterm.js does (it loads no image addon). The
#    handler wrote past its pixel buffer when an image's last run had no `$`/`-` (`\eP q#0~~\e\\`
#    CRASHED the process), trapped on an overflowing colour number (`#99999999999999999999`) and
#    allocated 24·n bytes for `!n` (review of 2026-09-27; testASixelImageIsIgnoredNotDecoded).
# 9. KITTY GRAPHICS (APC `G`) is never decoded, and no APC body is kept. A truncated zlib payload
#    (`\e_Ga=T,f=32,s=1,v=1,o=z;AAAA\e\\`) looped FOREVER in decompressZlib — on the host, holding the
#    tab's serial lock and TerminalManager.lock, freezing every tab and all input — and a zlib bomb
#    inflated without limit. SwiftTerm also ANSWERED kitty graphics queries xterm.js does not
#    (testKittyGraphicsIsIgnoredNotDecoded).
# 10. An OSC or DECRQSS body is CAPPED at xterm.js 6's PAYLOAD_LIMIT (10 000 000; it counts UTF-16 units,
#    this counts bytes, so a non-ASCII body is cut a little sooner). Past it the sequence is consumed but
#    DROPPED — not dispatched, not answered — as xterm.js drops it, and nothing more of it is kept: an
#    unterminated OSC grew the host's memory without bound (testAnOSCOrDCSBodyPastThePayloadLimitIsDropped).
# 11. CSI parameters and intermediates are CAPPED, as xterm.js 6 caps them (Params keeps 32 parameters
#    and 32 sub-parameters — 64 here, where SwiftTerm flattens both — and its collect is one 32-bit
#    number; 4 intermediates here). Past the cap further separators and digits are ignored and the
#    sequence still dispatches on its final. An UNTERMINATED CSI (`ESC [` then an endless run of `;`)
#    appended an Int per separator forever: 50 MB of `;` held 923 MB (measured, final review of
#    2026-09-27; testAnEndlessCSIDoesNotGrowMemory).
# And ONE RESIZE FIX (12) that DEPARTS from xterm.js on purpose:
# 12. A program's interface BELOW THE CURSOR survives a shrink. Buffer.resize (xterm.js's, line for line)
#    popped every line below the cursor — "a blank line below the cursor", it assumed — before scrolling
#    anything into history. An agent keeps its cursor in its input box with its own rule, footer and
#    update line UNDER it (Claude Code: the cursor 4 rows from the bottom, measured 2026-10-06), and on
#    SIGWINCH repaints by going back down to where its output ended — clamped at the last row once those
#    rows were popped — and erasing its own line count upward, which then erased that many CONVERSATION
#    lines (docs/terminal.md, "A program's interface below the cursor"). tmux does the same (its
#    `eat_empty`). Now, with ANY character below the cursor, the top scrolls into history and every row
#    below the cursor stays — a trailing blank row too, which the agent drew as well — down to the cursor
#    reaching the top row, past which the bottom is popped as before; with nothing below it, the blank
#    rows go as upstream (a fresh shell's prompt does not move). The client's held picture predicts the
#    same (web/src/client/hold-shift.ts, `drawnBelow`) (testAProgramsInterfaceBelowTheCursorSurvivesAShrink…).
# And `Unknown SGR attribute` and `TODO: Enable Locator Reporting (DECELR)` are logged through `log`,
# which honours `silentLog`, instead of `print` (they scribbled on the harness's stdout, which is a UI;
# the fuzz run printed the second 173 times).
#
# Nothing else changes. To regenerate: apply this patch to a pristine SwiftTerm v1.13.0 into b/,
# keep the pristine files in a/, edit b/, then append
#   (diff -ru a b)
# below this header — the header is every leading `#` line, so cut it by that, never by searching for
# "diff -ru" (the instruction above contains it; a regeneration that did lost these lines). MEASURED:
# two plain `diff -u` outputs concatenated are REFUSED by Bazel's patch parser (it applied
# Terminal.swift's last hunk to Buffer.swift); `diff -ru`'s form is accepted.
